<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/intermediate/shot-sizes-and-camera-grammar">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The shot is cinema\'s <strong>phoneme</strong> — size, angle, lens and movement each carry
    meaning before anyone speaks. Learning the vocabulary is learning to see WHY a frame feels
    the way it does: the grammar lesson, one level down.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="size, angle, lens, movement" widget="equationStep" config={shotCfg} />

  <h2>Reading and writing shots</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The 180° rule is spatial grammar:</strong> keep the camera on one side of the action line so screen direction stays consistent — the audience\'s mental map survives. Breaking it is legal when it means disorientation (battles, panic).</li>
    <li><strong>Shot/reverse-shot is conversation\'s engine:</strong> the pattern (A looking, B looking, eyelines matched) constructs a coherent space from fragments — and withholding one side (the unseen interviewer, a monster) spends suspense.</li>
    <li><strong>Coverage is production grammar:</strong> a scene shot in master, mediums and close-ups can be re-arranged in the edit — shot planning is pre-editing (the coming lesson\'s options bought on set).</li>
    <li><strong>Aspect ratio frames the argument:</strong> 4:3 boxes characters in (The Lighthouse), 2.39:1 widescreen isolates them in landscapes (Lawrence of Arabia) — the frame\'s shape is a tonal decision, not a technical leftover.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the grammar lesson; Bordwell & Thompson; the ASC interviews (American Cinematographer).</p>
</TopicPage>
